       "objectiveEn": "Explain how “Bulk placement logistics” governs forces, deformation, flow or signals in “Tarbela Dam”, and map the relevant inputs, transfer paths and boundaries.",
       "instruction": "塔貝拉的土石壩型式提供大量填築物流的學習背景，車輛載量、循環時間與有效工時共同限制日產量。理想車隊算例假設供料與攤鋪不成瓶頸，實際還需檢查材料分區、道路干擾與品質驗收速度，不能只加車就宣稱產能增加。 驗證時應核對過磅循環時間與攤鋪驗收紀錄並分別列鬆方實方，並辨識以下失效情境：把鬆方直接當完成實方會高估填築進度。這有助於確認模型的水流、受力或量測邊界是否仍成立。 師傅任務：以自由體圖、流路圖或訊號圖畫出相關物理量如何經過「大體積填築物流」傳到系統邊界，標出哪些量由設計決定、哪些由材料、施工或環境決定，再用三句話寫出機制摘要。",
       "instructionEn": "Tarbela provides context for large embankment placement logistics. Truck payload, cycle time and productive hours limit output. An ideal fleet estimate assumes loading and spreading are unconstrained; material zoning, road interference and acceptance capacity can become bottlenecks, so adding vehicles alone does not guarantee more placement. Test this interpretation with the following evidence: Reconcile haul cycles with placement acceptance and distinguish loose from compacted volume. In particular, distinguish this failure mechanism: Counting loose volume as completed compacted volume overstates progress. That distinction determines whether the model boundary or measurement assumption remains valid. Mentor task: draw a free-body, flow or signal diagram showing how relevant physical quantities pass through “Bulk placement logistics” to the system boundary, mark which quantities are fixed by design and which by materials, construction or environment, then summarise the mechanism in three sentences.",
       "observation": "量級提示：原創教學假設：trucks=20; payload=18 m3; cyclesPerHour=2; productiveHours=8; looseVolume=20*18*2*8=5760 m3/day。",
       "observationEn": "Order-of-magnitude cue: Original teaching assumptions: trucks=20; payload=18 m3; cyclesPerHour=2; productiveHours=8; looseVolume=20*18*2*8=5760 m3/day.",
       "failure": "常見誤解：「增加運輸車數就保證攤鋪完成量同比增加」這個說法忽略了上述機制；請在系統圖上指出它錯在哪一個環節。",
       "failureEn": "Common misconception: \"More haul trucks guarantee a proportional increase in accepted placement\" It ignores the mechanism above; mark on your system diagram where it breaks down.",
