       "objective": "說明「融雪脈衝與蓄量帳」如何影響黑部水壩的受力、變形、水流或訊號表現，並畫出相關輸入、傳遞路徑與邊界。",
       "objectiveEn": "Explain how “Snowmelt pulses and storage” governs forces, deformation, flow or signals in “Kurobe Dam”, and map the relevant inputs, transfer paths and boundaries.",
       "instruction": "高山積雪把降水暫存到融化季節，入庫峰值可能落後氣溫而非跟隨當日降雨。黑部背景的水量帳必須將庫區當作控制體，分別列出入流、放流與時段，蓄量增加後還需面積高程曲線才能換成水位。 融雪產流還受積雪含水量與土壤暫存控制，同一氣溫未必產生同一入流；庫內水量平衡只核算已到達的水。 師傅任務：以自由體圖、流路圖或訊號圖畫出相關物理量如何經過「融雪脈衝與蓄量帳」傳到系統邊界，標出哪些量由設計決定、哪些由材料、施工或環境決定，再用三句話寫出機制摘要。",
       "instructionEn": "Mountain snow delays runoff until melting, so an inflow peak can lag temperature rather than follow daily rain. A reservoir control volume separates inflow, release and duration; storage change still needs an area-elevation relationship before it becomes a level change. Snow water equivalent and temporary soil storage also control melt runoff. Identical air temperatures need not produce identical inflows; this reservoir balance accounts only for water that has already arrived. Mentor task: draw a free-body, flow or signal diagram showing how relevant physical quantities pass through “Snowmelt pulses and storage” to the system boundary, mark which quantities are fixed by design and which by materials, construction or environment, then summarise the mechanism in three sentences.",
       "observation": "量級提示：原創教學假設，以下數值不是本工程實測或設備設定：Qin=20 m3/s; Qout=12 m3/s; dt=21600 s; dV=(Qin-Qout)dt=172800 m3。",
       "observationEn": "Order-of-magnitude cue: Original teaching assumptions, not project measurements or equipment settings: Qin=20 m3/s; Qout=12 m3/s; dt=21600 s; dV=(Qin-Qout)dt=172800 m3.",
       "failure": "常見誤解：「蓄量增加必然讓水位以固定比例上升」這個說法忽略了上述機制；請在系統圖上指出它錯在哪一個環節。",
       "failureEn": "Common misconception: \"Every storage increase raises level in a fixed proportion\" It ignores the mechanism above; mark on your system diagram where it breaks down.",
       "check": "系統圖標明「融雪脈衝與蓄量帳」的輸入、傳遞路徑與邊界；三句摘要能解釋本步列出的誤解為何不成立。",
       "checkEn": "The system diagram shows inputs, transfer paths and boundaries for “Snowmelt pulses and storage”; the three-sentence summary explains why the listed misconception fails.",
