       "objectiveEn": "Explain how “Compound events and failure combinations” governs forces, deformation, flow or signals in “Marina Barrage”, and map the relevant inputs, transfer paths and boundaries.",
       "instruction": "暴雨、高海潮與電力限制可能同時發生，聯合機率取決於相關性。把各事件單獨罕見當成組合必定極罕見，會忽略共同氣象原因。 假設一年高海潮事件機率0.1，且該條件下強降雨機率0.3，聯合機率0.03；若誤用無條件雨率0.1獨立相乘會得到0.01。 樣本很少時條件機率不穩定，氣候變化又會改變共同分布。只列一個年機率而不說事件窗口，容易把短時設備容量與長期風險混為一談。 師傅任務：以自由體圖、流路圖或訊號圖畫出相關物理量如何經過「複合事件與失效組合」傳到系統邊界，標出哪些量由設計決定、哪些由材料、施工或環境決定，再用三句話寫出機制摘要。",
       "instructionEn": "Heavy rain, high tide and power constraints can coincide, with joint probability depending on dependence. Individually rare events do not necessarily make their combination extremely rare when weather drives several conditions together. Assume annual high-tide event probability 0.1 and conditional heavy-rain probability 0.3. Joint probability is 0.03; incorrectly multiplying an unconditional rain probability 0.1 gives 0.01. Conditional probabilities are unstable with sparse samples, and climate changes alter joint distributions. Reporting one annual probability without its event window confuses short-term capacity with long-term risk. Mentor task: draw a free-body, flow or signal diagram showing how relevant physical quantities pass through “Compound events and failure combinations” to the system boundary, mark which quantities are fixed by design and which by materials, construction or environment, then summarise the mechanism in three sentences.",
       "observation": "量級提示：教學假設，非地標實測值或操作設定：假設一年高海潮事件機率0.1，且該條件下強降雨機率0.3，聯合機率0.03；若誤用無條件雨率0.1獨立相乘會得到0.01。",
       "observationEn": "Order-of-magnitude cue: Teaching assumptions, not landmark measurements or operating settings: Assume annual high-tide event probability 0.1 and conditional heavy-rain probability 0.3.",
       "failure": "常見誤解：「罕見暴雨和罕見高潮可以不經檢查直接視為獨立。」這個說法忽略了上述機制；請在系統圖上指出它錯在哪一個環節。",
       "failureEn": "Common misconception: \"Rare rainfall and rare high tides can be assumed independent without checking.\" It ignores the mechanism above; mark on your system diagram where it breaks down.",
