       "objectiveEn": "Explain how “Reuse ratio and net freshwater use” governs forces, deformation, flow or signals in “Panama Canal expansion locks”, and map the relevant inputs, transfer paths and boundaries.",
       "instruction": "回用百分比必須有明確分母，閘室交換量、從湖取水量與全年水源消耗並不相同。漏流、蒸發與鹽分管理可能改變系統邊界內的淨耗用。 假設一次交換量200000 m³、回用率0.6、另有損失5000 m³，淡水補充=(1−0.6)200000+5000=85000 m³；非官方每次用水量。 只引用回用率而省略其他損失，會把單次水力效率誤當流域節水。閘次船型差異和不完整循環又可能造成看似改善卻不可比較的績效數字。 師傅任務：以自由體圖、流路圖或訊號圖畫出相關物理量如何經過「回用率與淨淡水耗用」傳到系統邊界，標出哪些量由設計決定、哪些由材料、施工或環境決定，再用三句話寫出機制摘要。",
       "instructionEn": "A reuse percentage requires an explicit denominator. Chamber exchange, lake withdrawal and annual water-resource consumption differ, while leakage, evaporation and salinity management can change net use within the chosen boundary. Assume cycle exchange 200000 m³, reuse fraction 0.6 and other losses 5000 m³. Freshwater makeup is (1−0.6)200000+5000=85000 m³, not an official lockage demand. Quoting reuse alone confuses cycle hydraulics with watershed savings. Vessel differences and incomplete cycles can produce apparently improved performance values that are not comparable across the measurement period. Mentor task: draw a free-body, flow or signal diagram showing how relevant physical quantities pass through “Reuse ratio and net freshwater use” to the system boundary, mark which quantities are fixed by design and which by materials, construction or environment, then summarise the mechanism in three sentences.",
       "observation": "量級提示：教學假設，非地標實測值或操作設定：假設一次交換量200000 m³、回用率0.6、另有損失5000 m³，淡水補充=(1−0.6)200000+5000=85000 m³；非官方每次用水量。",
       "observationEn": "Order-of-magnitude cue: Teaching assumptions, not landmark measurements or operating settings: Assume cycle exchange 200000 m³, reuse fraction 0.6 and other losses 5000 m³.",
       "failure": "常見誤解：「百分之六十回用一定讓全年淡水需求直接減少六成。」這個說法忽略了上述機制；請在系統圖上指出它錯在哪一個環節。",
       "failureEn": "Common misconception: \"Sixty percent reuse necessarily reduces annual freshwater demand by sixty percent.\" It ignores the mechanism above; mark on your system diagram where it breaks down.",
